.TH MAILCONN 2
.SH NAME
mailconn \- a mail server's connection, with deadlines and bounds
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "keyring.m";
include "tls.m";
include "mailconn.m";
mailconn := load Mailconn Mailconn->PATH;
Link: import mailconn;

CLEAR, IMPLICIT, IMAPSTARTTLS, SMTPSTARTTLS: con iota;
STOPPED: con "stopped";

init: fn(): string;

Link: adt {
    err:      string;
    dial:     fn(addr, servername: string, how: int,
                 helo: string, ms: int): (ref Link, string);
    deadline: fn(c: self ref Link, idlems, totalms: int);
    getline:  fn(c: self ref Link, max: int): (string, string);
    waitline: fn(c: self ref Link, max: int,
                 stop: chan of int): (string, string);
    getbytes: fn(c: self ref Link, n: int): (array of byte, string);
    write:    fn(c: self ref Link, b: array of byte): string;
    close:    fn(c: self ref Link);
};
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Mailconn
is the connection
.IR smtp (2),
.IR pop3 (2)
and the IMAP client of
.IR mail9p (4)
speak their protocols on.
A server is never waited for without limit, and is never taken at
its word about how much it will send.
.PP
.B Init
loads what the module needs and returns nil, or why it could not.
.PP
.B Link.dial
connects to
.I addr
(a dial string,
.BI tcp! host ! port \fR)
within
.I ms
milliseconds, the TLS handshake included, and returns the connection,
or nil and the reason
.RB ( "timed out"
at the deadline).
.I How
is
.B CLEAR
for a connection with no TLS;
.B IMPLICIT
for TLS from the first byte;
.B IMAPSTARTTLS
for IMAP's greeting and STARTTLS in clear and then TLS;
.B SMTPSTARTTLS
for SMTP's greeting,
.B EHLO
.I helo
and STARTTLS in clear and then TLS.
A server that does not offer STARTTLS, or refuses it, is refused:
nothing else is sent in clear.
TLS is
.IR tls (2)'s
client with its default configuration: the server's chain is verified
against the system's roots, and
.I servername
against its certificate.
.PP
.B Deadline
sets the limits for the reads and writes that follow:
.I idlems
milliseconds with nothing moving, and
.I totalms
in all from now; 0 is no such limit.
.PP
.B Getline
returns the next line, without its CR LF or LF.
A line of more than
.I max
bytes is an error.
.B Waitline
is
.B getline
with no deadline, for a caller that waits on a server by design
(IMAP's IDLE): it returns the error
.B STOPPED
when a value arrives on
.IR stop ,
leaving the connection as it was.
.B Getbytes
returns exactly
.I n
bytes; its memory is taken as the bytes arrive, so a count costs
nothing until it is kept.
.B Write
writes
.IR b .
.PP
Each returns an error string, nil if there is none.
After any error but
.BR STOPPED
\(em a timeout, the server's hanging up, a line too long, a failed
write \(em the connection is closed and
.B err
holds the reason, which every later call returns.
.B Close
closes it: its processes go and the server is hung up on.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/mailconn.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR pop3 (2),
.IR smtp (2),
.IR tls (2),
.IR mail9p (4)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.B "timed out"
(a deadline passed),
.B "connection closed"
(the server hung up),
.B "line too long" ,
.BR "write failed: " ...,
and from
.BR dial :
.BR "dial " ...,
.BR "tlsdial: tls: " ...,
.BR "STARTTLS refused: " ...,
.BR "STARTTLS not offered by " ....
